 ],
 "literal": "Like the feathers of a fish-eating heron / the old woman with pure white grey hair, her son / having struck down a male elephant, died, saying so, the joy / was greater than the day she gave birth to him; her tears / on the strong-stalked swaying bamboo / than the drops suspended when the sky rained, are indeed many.",
 "translation": "The joy of the old woman, whose hair is as white as the feathers of a fish-eating heron, upon hearing that her son fell in battle after slaying a war elephant, was even greater than the day she gave birth to him. Yet, her tears of pride and grief are more numerous than the raindrops clinging to the swaying stalks of strong bamboo after a downpour.",
 "modern": "மீனை உண்ணும் கொக்கின் இறகைப் போன்ற தூய நரைமுடியைக் கொண்ட முதியவளின் மகன், போர்க்களத்தில் ஆண் யானையை வீழ்த்தித் தானும் மடிந்தான் என்ற செய்தியைக் கேட்டபோது அவளுக்கு ஏற்பட்ட மகிழ்ச்சி, அவனைப் பெற்றெடுத்த நாளில் அடைந்த மகிழ்ச்சியை விடப் பெரியதாகும். அவளது கண்களிலிருந்து பெருகிய கண்ணீர்த்துளிகள், பலத்த மூங்கில் காடுகளில் மழை பெய்த பிறகு அசையும் மூங்கில் கணுக்களில் தங்கியிருக்கும் நீர்த்துளிகளை விடப் பலவாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the tumpai tiṇai, which deals with battle and heroic deeds, and specifically illustrates the theme of uvakaik kaluḻcci, or weeping out of overwhelming joy and pride. The poet Pūṅkaṇuttiraiyār describes an aged mother whose hair is as white as the feathers of a fish-eating heron. When she hears that her son died heroically on the battlefield after slaying an enemy war elephant, she is filled with a pride that surpasses the joy of the day he was born. However, her maternal grief and pride manifest as tears that flow uncontrollably. The poet compares her tears to the countless water droplets clinging to and falling from swaying bamboo stalks after a heavy rain, beautifully capturing the complex mixture of sorrow and triumph.",
